{% doc %}
  Emits padding as custom properties, for a section or for a block.

  ## Class or attribute — both work

  `base.css` matches either:

      .section-spacing        /  .block-spacing         write it yourself
      [data-section-spacing]  /  [data-block-spacing]   emitted below

  Reach for the class in new markup. This snippet keeps emitting the attribute
  because it cannot merge a class into an element that already has a `class`
  attribute — two `class` attributes on one tag and the browser takes the first,
  silently dropping every other class on the section root.

  Neither hook involves JavaScript. `[data-*]` in a stylesheet is an ordinary
  attribute selector: same cascade, same cost as a class, nothing reading it at
  runtime.

  ## The hook is the whole point

  `base.css` applies this padding through attribute selectors:

      [data-section-spacing] {
        padding-block-start: var(--section-padding-block-start, 0px);
        padding-block-end:   var(--section-padding-block-end, 0px);
      }

  This snippet emitted only the `style` attribute. The custom properties landed
  on elements that no rule ever selected, so every padding slider in the theme
  editor moved a value nothing read — present in the UI, dead on the page, in
  sections and blocks alike. The attribute is what connects them.

  ## Two property sets, not one

  A section pads block-start and block-end and takes an explicit mobile
  override. A block pads all four sides and scales the desktop value down on
  small screens by a percentage. Different shapes, so different property names,
  matching what `base.css` reads:

      --section-padding-block-start / -end          (+ `-mobile` variants)
      --block-padding-block-start / -end / -inline-start / -inline-end
      --block-padding-scale-mobile

  ## Truthiness, not blank comparisons

  Every guard here is either an `if object` test or a number coerced with
  `| default: 0 | plus: 0`. Neither depends on how a Liquid engine compares an
  absent value to `blank` — and that comparison is exactly where this went
  wrong before: an unset `padding_top_mobile` passed the blank test and emitted
  `--section-padding-block-start-mobile:px`, an invalid declaration that takes
  the whole rule down with it.

  A hash is truthy and nil is falsy in every Liquid, so `if section` is a
  reliable "was a section passed" test where a blank comparison is not.

  ## Zero values are omitted, the attribute is not

  A declaration is only written when the value is non-zero: the fallbacks in
  `base.css` already resolve to `0px`, so writing `0px` adds bytes to every
  block on the page to restate the stylesheet. The attribute is emitted either
  way, because a merchant can drag a slider back to zero and the element still
  has to be selectable.

  Left and right are spelled `inline-start` and `inline-end` so they follow the
  text direction and flip in right-to-left languages.

  Parameters:
    section  (object, optional)  A section settings object
    block    (object, optional)  A block settings object

  Pass one or the other. `settings` is not accepted — it is a global drop in
  Shopify, so a snippet sees it whether or not the caller passed it, and using
  it as a fallback made every section claim to be a block as well.

  @example
    <div {% render 'spacing', block: block.settings %}>

  @example
    <section {% render 'spacing', section: section.settings %}>
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  comment
    `settings` is deliberately NOT accepted as an alias for `block`.

    In Shopify Liquid, `settings` is a global drop — the theme's own settings —
    and a snippet sees globals even when the caller passes nothing. So
    `block | default: settings` never fell through to nil on a section call: it
    fell through to the *theme settings object*, which is always truthy. Every
    section root in the theme came out with both hooks on it:

      class="announcement-bar section-spacing …"
      data-section-spacing data-block-spacing

    and the stray one is not cosmetic. `[data-block-spacing]` sets all four
    paddings to `var(--block-padding-*, 0px)`, so it overrode whatever padding
    the section's own stylesheet gave it with zero — on every section on the
    page. The one caller that used the alias, `snippets/group.liquid`, now
    passes `block:` instead.

    The two scopes are mutually exclusive by construction below rather than by
    the caller remembering to pass only one.
  endcomment
  assign is_section = false
  assign is_block = false

  if section
    assign is_section = true
  elsif block
    assign is_block = true
  endif

  assign b = block

  assign out = ''

  if is_section
    assign s_top = section.padding_top | default: 0 | plus: 0
    assign s_bottom = section.padding_bottom | default: 0 | plus: 0
    assign s_top_mobile = section.padding_top_mobile | default: 0 | plus: 0
    assign s_bottom_mobile = section.padding_bottom_mobile | default: 0 | plus: 0

    if s_top > 0
      assign out = out | append: '--section-padding-block-start:' | append: s_top | append: 'px;'
    endif
    if s_bottom > 0
      assign out = out | append: '--section-padding-block-end:' | append: s_bottom | append: 'px;'
    endif

    comment
      Mobile is only emitted when the merchant set it. Left at 0 the stylesheet
      falls back to the desktop value, which is what the "leave at 0 to use the
      desktop value" hint on those settings promises.
    endcomment
    if s_top_mobile > 0
      assign out = out | append: '--section-padding-block-start-mobile:' | append: s_top_mobile | append: 'px;'
    endif
    if s_bottom_mobile > 0
      assign out = out | append: '--section-padding-block-end-mobile:' | append: s_bottom_mobile | append: 'px;'
    endif
  endif

  if is_block
    comment
      Both spellings are accepted. Most blocks use padding_top; a few newer ones
      use the CSS-shaped padding-block-start. Reading either means a block does
      not have to be rewritten to gain working padding.

      The default filter does not replace 0, only nil, false and empty string —
      so a merchant's deliberate 0 survives the chain rather than falling
      through to the other spelling.
    endcomment
    assign b_top = b.padding_top | default: b['padding-block-start'] | default: 0 | plus: 0
    assign b_bottom = b.padding_bottom | default: b['padding-block-end'] | default: 0 | plus: 0
    assign b_left = b.padding_left | default: b['padding-inline-start'] | default: 0 | plus: 0
    assign b_right = b.padding_right | default: b['padding-inline-end'] | default: 0 | plus: 0
    assign b_scale = b.padding_scale_mobile | default: 0 | plus: 0

    if b_top > 0
      assign out = out | append: '--block-padding-block-start:' | append: b_top | append: 'px;'
    endif
    if b_bottom > 0
      assign out = out | append: '--block-padding-block-end:' | append: b_bottom | append: 'px;'
    endif
    if b_left > 0
      assign out = out | append: '--block-padding-inline-start:' | append: b_left | append: 'px;'
    endif
    if b_right > 0
      assign out = out | append: '--block-padding-inline-end:' | append: b_right | append: 'px;'
    endif

    comment
      The setting is a percentage; the stylesheet multiplies by it, so it has to
      arrive as a unitless fraction.
    endcomment
    if b_scale > 0
      assign scale = b_scale | divided_by: 100.0
      assign out = out | append: '--block-padding-scale-mobile:' | append: scale | append: ';'
    endif
  endif
-%}

{%- if is_section -%}data-section-spacing {% endif -%}
{%- if is_block -%}data-block-spacing {% endif -%}
{%- if out != blank %}style="{{ out }}"{% endif -%}
